PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I continue to favor E after weighing the latest clarifications, but I want to sharpen the argument around the selective, C-dependent pattern rather than any blanket claim that the ΔAΔB difference is invisible. The decisive signature remains that only the ΔC single mutant produces identical loads (3000/ml) in both host lines; this equalization occurs precisely when C is removed and nowhere else. In every other background where C is still present, the host-genotype differential either fails to appear (single ΔA or ΔB) or appears only under the specific combinatorial condition of losing both A and B together.

This pattern is most economically explained if C is the factor whose activity is directly coupled to counteracting xy, while A and B exert secondary or combinatorial effects that become relevant only once the primary C–xy interaction is eliminated. The triple-mutant data reinforce the point: the further drop to 1000/ml is confined to wtL and is additive to the 3000/ml baseline already set by ΔC alone. If A and B acted as fully independent, redundant neutralizers of xy regardless of C status, we would expect their combined loss to generate a consistent host-genotype gap even when C remains intact; instead the gap materializes or changes magnitude only after C deletion.

E therefore accounts for the full dataset without requiring an additional assumption that the A/B redundancy on xy is conditionally masked or superseded by C. The F model’s insistence on unconditional A/B–xy redundancy forces us to posit an extra masking mechanism that the triple-mutant counts contradict, whereas E’s selective C–xy tie explains every observed differential with fewer postulates. I remain open to any reconciliation that preserves the redundancy model while explaining why the ΔC equalization is so uniquely complete, but the current evidence still points most cleanly to C as the primary xy interactor.}

